{"title":"Count Subsequences Greater Than T","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"Given two strings `s` and `t` of lowercase English letters, count the subsequences of `s` that are **lexicographically strictly greater** than `t`, and print the count modulo `10^9 + 7`.\n\nA subsequence is obtained by deleting zero or more characters of `s` without changing the order of the rest. Subsequences are distinguished by the **set of chosen positions**, not by their text: if the same string can be formed from different positions, each choice is counted separately. The empty subsequence is not greater than any non-empty `t`.\n\nString `u` is lexicographically greater than `t` if at the first position where they differ `u` has the larger character, or if `t` is a proper prefix of `u`.","inputFormat":"The first line contains the string `s`.\nThe second line contains the string `t`.","outputFormat":"Print a single integer: the number of subsequences of `s` strictly greater than `t`, modulo 10^9 + 7.","constraints":["1 ≤ |s|, |t| ≤ 1000","s and t consist of lowercase English letters"],"examples":[{"input":"aba\nab","output":"3","explanation":"The subsequences of `aba` by position are a, b, a, ab, aa, ba, aba. Only `b`, `ba` and `aba` are strictly greater than `ab`, so the answer is 3."},{"input":"abc\nb","output":"2","explanation":"The strictly greater subsequences are `c` and `bc`. `b` is equal to `t`, and `a`, `ab`, `ac`, `abc` start with a smaller letter."},{"input":"zz\na","output":"3","explanation":"`z` (first position), `z` (second position) and `zz` are all greater than `a`."}],"tests":[{"input":"a\na","output":"0"},{"input":"b\na","output":"1"},{"input":"a\nb","output":"0"},{"input":"aaa\naa","output":"1"},{"input":"ab\nabc","output":"1"},{"input":"abc\nab","output":"5"},{"input":"zzzzzz\nzzz","output":"22"},{"input":"cb\ndada","output":"0"},{"input":"ba\naa","output":"2"},{"input":"aaa\naaaa","output":"0"},{"input":"cdd\ncbdacb","output":"6"},{"input":"abdb\nd","output":"1"},